earlier today how worried are you that  your region is heading for tier three  well we always always review the  evidence kay always and we'll take  whatever decision  is needed to protect our residents back  in the summer we took a very difficult  decision when the government asked  us to go under restrictions and we did  that but  you know we're in a very different  position to the liverpool city region i  was surprised  to hear all of yesterday the prime  minister and other ministers saying we  were effectively the same we're not  uh in liverpool uh some of the boroughs  have over 600  cases per hundred thousand we we don't  we we don't have anything near that  and the average number of cases here is  quite a lot lower as is the number of  people  uh going into hospital so it's a  different picture but we're not  complacent k  we remain vigilant and we will do what  is needed to protect people  how much pressure are you under from  westminster downing street particularly  in order to move in  to tier three  yes there is uh pressure and it was  certainly piled on  uh yesterday but we have uh serious  doubts  about tier three and let me just explain  what they are two main ones  okay number one there is not a proper  economic support package  not just a local furlough scheme that is  comprehensive but also  no scheme for people who are  self-employed in a wider business  support scheme so  to go into tier three is to accept  significant harm  to the economy and we are not prepared  to do that  in any way lightly but the second  problem is we don't think the evidence  is there  to say that the tier three measures will  get results and actually  the chief medical officer said the same  last night  so this is where this takes me kay in  terms of my  thinking you know is it going to be  better to have  large parts of the country suffering  under tier 3 with all of the economic  harm that that does  with no real results coming from it  which is basically what we were told  last night  or would it be better not to do that and  at some point consider  a circuit break i certainly would prefer  that the latter  rather than places being forced into  tier three  all of the economic damage that comes  with that but without the real results  in tackling the virus and i think that's  the question  for the government this morning so you  would support a national rather than a  regional lockdown  a circuit break uh as proposed by  uh sage i think is preferable  uh to a situation where the government  one by one pile the pressure on local  areas to go into tier three sort of  grind them down and force them into tier  three  as i say that will be a path to  hardship for people redundancies  business failure  now why would we do that lightly why  would we just sort of roll over and  accept that we're not going to do  to do that and what i'm saying is if if  the alternative is  a shorter circuit break then certainly i  would i would much prefer that and i  would support  that over this sort of process of  putting people into tier three and  letting them  suffer away without of the economic  support that they are going to need  we were given the impression by the  mayor of liverpool and the  city region that they didn't really have  much choice and they found themselves in  tier three um they tried to resist they  didn't feel that they were necessarily  supported  by the government as much as they would  want to be what if you're  pushed into it what are you going to do  with your backs against the wall  well i think they're right because i i  obviously have been  privy to some of those conversations  they were given no choice and that's  really important to say we were  it was conceded in our meeting with  downing street that we were in a  different position  to liverpool so i am slightly uh  frustrated at some of the language they  came out with  yesterday and having put in our  proposals to the government  over the weekend we didn't hear anything  back and we were just told we were in  tier two  yesterday so there was no no negotiation  no real engagement so  i don't know that's a strange way i  think for the for the government to go  about things we are in a serious  situation but the question is what will  most work  whilst causing the least damage to the  economy of the north of england and i  think the whole construct of  tier three without that proper uh  economic package coming with it is going  to level down large parts of the north  if we just go with it  and that is why i am arguing back  against it  uh this morning the the government  either changes the support it provides  uh or it considers an alternative route  and i think that basically is where  we're at this morning  yeah i'm asking i suppose on behalf of  my viewers in greater manchester this  morning  if you if their mayor is forced by the  government to go into tier three what  will he do  will he push back and say i'm not going  to do that or will you accept it but  but i believe it's within your  jurisdiction to say  yeah but i'm not going to close the bars  and i'm not going to close the pubs and  i'm not going to close the gyms you can  make that decision  on a regional level is that your  understanding as well and if so is that  what you would do  well i would say to the residents of  great manchester it's not just  me alone and i'm meeting with the ten  council leaders uh this morning to  discuss  uh the situation and we will give it  very careful consideration and  particularly the seriousness  of the health situation we want measures  okay that are going to work  if we do them if we take the pain at  least let's get the results  and the chief medic medical officer told  the country last night that tier three  was pain without results and we're  we're not in favor of that kind of  approach so we will discuss it and we'll  discuss it  carefully and we will do what's right  for greater manchester and its residents  what we don't think is right is to just  accept  that cave into the pressure from the  government accept tier 3  and then expose our residents to all of  the uh the hardship that would come  with that so you know we will stand our  ground we we  act on the evidence at all times uh  we've we've called  uh things often correctly throughout  this you know to hear robert generick  saying oh it's wonderful  you know what councils can do contact  racing this morning he's always come up  with a great idea  we have been saying that all year and he  hasn't been  listening so i'm afraid you know we're  not going to just be  kind of told what to do by the  government we will take our own  decisions this morning  and then we will enter discussions with  the government but as i say  large parts of the poorest parts of the  country  suffering under tier 3 or winter is not  something that appeals to me and the  world health organization  said last week there was one sure thing  about lockdowns they make the forest in  society even poorer  and i'm afraid the government has not  come up with measures that will stop  that happening  yeah i suppose what i'm looking for mr  burnham's clarification for the people  that you are representing that's  obviously why you've come to talk to us  this morning are you saying if downing  street tries to do something in the  northwest that you feel is detrimental  to the health and economic well-being of  the people that you represent you will  ignore them  no i'm not saying that because in the  end the government can impose  and they can impose the law on us so no  i'm not going to get into a pointless  you know gesture politics  what you can do is it is then left up to  the regional decisions is my  understanding i may be wrong that you  can then decide  whether what level of lockdown that  might be in whether the pubs need to  close and  whether the restaurants need to close  and whether or not uh we need to  see gyms closing and that sort of thing  is that yo