down ever since and i think that is when  the story of this is told hey  he's three times higher than it was uh  in london and  i think that explains why the north has  struggled and you know those politicians  who seek to blame the people here  is an easy way of uh of kind of shifting  blame for themselves  i'm afraid got their answer from the  deputy chief medical officer yesterday  but could you not have imposed  a regional lockdown as we saw regional  lockdowns in leicester and the light was  that not your role to do that  we did so we've had a lockdown for 10  weeks  and so the idea being kind of put out  yesterday by the government that we are  somehow weak and we won't take the tough  decisions well  i'm saying we took them 10 weeks ago and  our residents have been under tier 2  style restrictions for all of that  time and that is a big ask of them  actually and to kind of minimize that  i i think is wrong for the government to  to do that so we we've taken those  tough decisions we're just of the view  that we think these  uh halfway house lockdowns of the kind  being proposed in tier two and tier 3  have limited effectiveness i think they  dampen the figures  but they don't bring them back down the  other way i think only  a circuit break would do that and then  out of that a fully functioning  localized test and trace system okay  very quick thought before i let you go  mr mayor  you were previously health secretary of  course i've grilled you many times in  that role how much sympathy do you have  for matt hancock at the moment  well i've said all year that i do you  know no one envies the position that the  government  is in um it's a tough place for them to  be  but in the last few days that sympathy  has won very thin  because they've just been they are  handing down judgments briefing  newspapers  not working truly in partnership with us  you can't  tell everyone who works in pubs in the  north of england that you know 10 p.m on  a wednesday night tha